<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="topic-1-1-purpose">
  <time minutes="45" />
  <title>Purpose and Behavior</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>Computer Science is an exciting field that is transforming the way we live and work. 
      Watch the <url
      href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QvyTEx1wyOY">video</url> below to learn more about how
      computers and software are changing the world.
    </p>
    <video youtube="QvyTEx1wyOY" label="video-cs-everywhere" />
    <p>      
      In this lessons, we will explore the purpose and behavior of apps by interacting with them and 
      observing their input and output. 
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection>
    <title>Purpose and Behavior of Apps</title>

    <p>Think about your favorite apps on your phone and their purpose. <term>App</term> is short for <term>application</term>, 
      which is a program that runs on a computer or any computing device including your phone.</p>
  
      <note>
      <title>1.1.A.1</title>
      <p><term>Applications (apps)</term> are programs that run on many kinds of computing devices.
        Apps can run on smartphones, tablets, laptops, desktops, game consoles, and web browsers,
        and they are designed to help users accomplish tasks or goals.
      </p>
    </note>


    <note>
      <title>1.1.A.2</title>
      <p>
        People use apps to help solve real problems and accomplish everyday tasks,
        such as communicating, learning, navigating, creating and sharing media, staying organized,
        and being entertained.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>An app's <term>purpose</term> is why it was created, its ultimate goal or the problem it solves. 
      For example, the purpose of a texting app on a phone is to help us to communicate with each other.</p>

    <p>Take a look at the <url
        href="https://www.google.com/search?q=stopwatch">Google Stopwatch</url> or 
        <url
        href="https://dwyl.github.io/stopwatch/">Github Stopwatch app</url> below. 
        Why would someone use this app? What is its purpose? 
        What happens when you click the Start button? What happens when you click the Pause or Reset buttons?
        Try it out below.
      </p>
        <interactive iframe="https://dwyl.github.io/stopwatch/" width="100%" aspect="2.5:1"/>

    <activity label="stopwatchPurpose">
  <statement>
    <p>What is the purpose of the <url
        href="https://www.google.com/search?q=stopwatch">Google Stopwatch app</url>?</p>
  </statement>
  <choices  randomize="yes">
    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p>To tell time</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>That would be a watch or clock app.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
    <choice>
      <statement>
          <p>To time an event by counting down from a set amount of time</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>A timer counts down from a set amount of time. A stopwatch counts up from zero. </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>To measure how much time has passed starting from zero</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>Correct! A stopwatch keeps track of how much time has passed starting from zero, such as timing a race or an activity.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p>To perform calculations</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>Calculations are done with a calculator app, not a stopwatch.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
  </choices>
</activity>
   
    <note>
      <title>1.1.A.3</title>
      <p>An app is successful when it uses information to produce an output that helps a user
        achieve a goal. An app’s value to the user depends on how well the results meet their needs.
      </p>
    </note>
    <note>
      <title>1.1.A.4</title>
      <p>Because people rely on apps for everyday tasks, app behavior matters.
        When an app is confusing, unreliable, or does not work as expected, users can become
        frustrated because it interrupts what they are trying to do.
      </p>
    </note>
 <p>Some apps are easy to use with clear purposes and behaviors, but sometimes apps and other designs can
      fail to meet user needs or expectations. Watch the following 
      <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qtCEoGyfsxk">video</url> about bad design choices.</p>

     <video youtube="qtCEoGyfsxk" label="video-bad-door-design" />

   <p>Take a look at this <url
        href="https://goulartnogueira.github.io/BadUI/Date/BadUIDate.html">app with bad design</url> below
        or the <url href="https://userinyerface.com/">userinyerface.com</url> funny website. 
        The purpose of the app is to accept your date of birth, but the app is not a successful design. Try to put in
        your birthdate. Is it frustrating to use? 
      </p>
        <interactive iframe="https://goulartnogueira.github.io/BadUI/Date/BadUIDate.html" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>

      <activity label="bad-design">
      <statement>
        <p>What are the issues with the <url
        href="https://goulartnogueira.github.io/BadUI/Date/BadUIDate.html">Bad Date app</url> 
        and how does it behave when you interact with it? How could it be improved to better meet user needs and expectations?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <response />
    </activity>
    
    <note>
      <title>1.1.A.5</title>

      <p>An app’s <term>behavior</term> is what it does while it is running, including how it responds to user
        interactions.
        The behavior of an app is determined by its program code.</p>
    </note>
    <note>
      <title>1.1.A.6</title>
      <p>An app should behave as intended across a range of conditions,
        including different ways people may interact with it.</p>
    </note>
    <note>
      <title>1.1.A.7</title>
      <p>Users can understand an app’s purpose and behavior by using it and observing how it
        responds to their actions.
      </p>
    </note>

    <activity label="google-maps">
      <statement>
        <p>Take a look at <url
        href="https://maps.google.com">Google Maps</url>. What is the purpose of Google Maps?
       People may interact with Google Maps in different ways. 
       Describe the behavior of Google Maps including how you interact with it and how it responds
       to do two different tasks.
      </p>
      </statement>
      <response />
    </activity>

  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Inputs and Outputs</title>

    <p>Watch the following <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mCq8-xTH7jA">video</url> about what 
      makes a computer a computer.</p>
    
      <video youtube="mCq8-xTH7jA" label="video-computer" />

    <note>
      <title>1.1.B.1</title>
      <p>Apps are designed to take in information from a variety of sources, process that
        information, and respond in useful ways.</p>
    </note>

      <p>As seen in the video, all computing devices and the apps that run on them are created to receive <term>input</term> 
        from users, store it as data, process the data, and produce <term>output</term> to communicate with users.</p>

    <note>
      <title>1.1.B.2</title>
      <p>When users interact with apps, they often tap or click buttons, type letters or numbers,
        speak, or move the device.
        Each of these actions provides <term>input</term>: data sent to a computer for a program to process.</p>
    </note>  
    
    <image source="_static/Unit1images/phoneIO.png" alt="Inputs and Outputs" width="50%" />

    <p><term>Input</term> is data sent to a computer for processing by a program, and <term>output</term> is the 
      data sent back from the program to the device. 
      Input and output can come in many forms, such as tactile (for example touching a button or the device 
      vibrating), audible (a sound), visual (an image), or text. </p>

   <activity label="stopwatchIO">
  <statement>
    <p>Which of the following are examples of input and output in the <url
        href="https://www.google.com/search?q=stopwatch">Google Stopwatch app</url>?</p>
  </statement>
  <choices  randomize="yes">
    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>Tapping the Start button</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>Correct! Tapping a button is user input.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p>The system time</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>The system time is used by the stopwatch app to calculate the elapsed time but it is not input or output to the user.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>The elapsed time displayed on the screen</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>Correct! The app displays the elapsed time as output.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
    <choice>
      <statement>The color of the displayed time</statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>The color of the displayed time is set by the app code but it is not input or output to the stopwatch app.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>Pressing the reset button</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>Pressing the reset button is an input to the stopwatch app.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
  </choices>
</activity>

    <note>
      <title>1.1.B.3</title>
      <p>Apps should provide clear feedback when they receive input, such as highlighting a button,
        showing a loading icon, playing a sound, vibrating, or displaying an error message.
        This feedback is a form of output that helps users know the app received the input, is
        working, and what step to take next.</p>
    </note>

    <p>The door design video above mentioned feedback in human-centered design, where feedback tells the user that
      something happened after their input. 
    </p>

    <note>
      <title>1.1.B.4</title>
      <p>Input often comes from a user’s direct actions, but it can also be captured automatically
        from sources such as sensors (like an accelerometer), location, time, or network signals.
        Apps may also receive audio or images from microphones and cameras, often through user
        actions (like a voice command or face scan).
        These inputs allow apps to provide features like maps, step counting, face filters, or
        recommendations.</p>
    </note>
   
   <activity label="tiltVibrationExample">
  <statement>
    <p>Which app below is most likely to use tilting the device (accelerometer) as an input or vibration as an output?</p>
  </statement>
  <choices  randomize="yes">
    <choice correct="yes">
      <statement>
        <p>A car racing game</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>Yes, a car racing game could use tilting the phone to steer the car and phone vibration as output during a crash. 
         </p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p>A calculator app that adds two numbers</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>A calculator typically uses taps as input and text as output, not tilt or vibration.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p>A tic tac toe game</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>Tic tac toe games mainly use touch input and visual output.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
    <choice>
      <statement>
        <p>A weather app that displays today's forecast</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>Weather apps primarily display information on the screen rather than using tilt or vibration.</p>
      </feedback>
    </choice>
  </choices>
</activity>

     <activity label="automatic-input">
      <statement>
        <p>Describe a device or app that uses input from a sensor or camera. If you cannot think of one,
        search online for examples. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <response /> 
      </activity>

    <note>
      <title>1.1.B.5</title>
      <p>In many apps, the program responds to <term>events</term>, such as button clicks, key presses, or sensor
        updates, by running specific code.
        Events are one way input is delivered to a program. An event signals that something
        happened, and an <term>event handler</term> processes the input and determines what happens next.</p>
    </note>

    <p>Most apps these days use <term>event-driven programming</term>, which means they display the 
      <term>user interface (UI)</term> and wait for <term>user events</term> to trigger actions, 
      for example for the user to touch a button. 
In event-driven programming, the code is attached to input such as button click and only run when triggered. 
The app's behavior is determined by the code that runs in response to the input.
</p>
    <note>
      <title>1.1.B.6</title>
      <p><term>Outputs</term> are what an app produces. Outputs may include text, images, sounds, or something
        the user feels, like a vibration.
        Some outputs are directly perceived by the user, while others are not, such as data written
        to a file or sent over a network.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.1.B.7</title>
      <p>Users can identify inputs and outputs by trying different actions in an app and observing
        what the app displays or does in response.
      </p>
    </note>

 <p>Try the following pizza web app by typing in your name and choosing different options. 
      Think about the inputs and outputs to the app. </p>

    <interactive iframe="webapps/pizzaApp.html" width="100%" aspect="2.5:1.2" />

    <activity label="pizzaAppIO">
      <statement>
        <p>Interact with the pizza app above. What are the inputs and outputs for this app?</p>
      </statement>
      <response />
    </activity>

    
    <activity label="pizzaAppOutputTypes">
      <statement>
        <p>Which of the following output types does the pizza app above use? Check all that apply.</p>  
      </statement>
      <choices  randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>text</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>The app prints out text output when the button is clicked.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>vibration</statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>This app cannot vibrate.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>image</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Pizza images are displayed as output if the user likes pizza.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>sound</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>There is no sound with this app.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice> 
          <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>color</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Yes, the background color changes as a result of user interactions.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice> 
      </choices>
    </activity>

  </subsection>


    <subsection xml:id="LOs-1-1">
    <title>Learning Objectives Review</title>

    <p>After this lesson, you should be able to:</p>

    <remark>
      <title>1.1.A</title>
      <p>
        Determine the purpose and behavior of an app by interacting with it.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>1.1.B</title>
      <p>
        Identify inputs and outputs of an app while using it.
      </p>
    </remark>

  </subsection>
 <subsection xml:id="vocab-1-1">
     <title>Vocabulary Review</title>

    <activity label="vocab-IO">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Review the vocabulary in this lesson. Drag the vocabulary term from the left and drop it
          on its correct definition on the right.
          Click the "Check Me" button to see if you are correct.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>Find the words in the lesson above and try again.</p>
      </feedback>

      <cardsort>
        <match>
          <premise>app</premise>
          <response>a program that runs on a computer or any computing device</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>purpose</premise>
          <response>why an app exists or its goal</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>behavior</premise>
          <response>what an app does when used</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>input</premise>
          <response>data sent to a computer for a program to process.</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>output</premise>
          <response>data sent back from a program to the device or user</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>user interface (UI)</premise>
          <response>the part of an app (for example buttons) that users directly interact with</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>event</premise>
          <response>a signal that something happened, such as a button click or key press</response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>event handler</premise>
          <response>code that runs in response to an event</response>
        </match>
      </cardsort>
    </activity>
  </subsection>
</section>